items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:29:---
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:30:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:31:## Statement
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:32:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:33:Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ with $m \ge 1$, with vector-valued functions, their
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:34:components $f_i = \pi_i \circ f$, their limits and their continuity as in
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:35:[[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]].
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:36:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:37:1. **Continuity is componentwise.** Let $(X,d_X)$ be a metric space, $A \subseteq X$,
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:38:   $f : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ and $a \in A$. Then $f$ is continuous at $a$ if and
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:39:   only if every component $f_i : A \to \mathbb{R}$ $(i<m)$ is continuous at $a$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:40:2. **Limits are componentwise.** Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, let $c$ be a limit
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:41:   point of $A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ and let
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:42:   $L \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$. Then $\lim_{x\to c} f(x) = L$ if and only if
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:43:   $\lim_{x\to c} f_i(x) = L_i$ for every $i<m$ ([[def-function-limit]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:44:3. **Algebra.** Let $(X,d_X)$, $A$, $a$ be as in clause 1, let
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:45:   $f, g : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous at $a$ and let $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:46:   Then $f + g$ and $\lambda f$ (defined pointwise) are continuous at $a$; the
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:47:   real-valued function $x \mapsto \langle f(x), g(x)\rangle$ is continuous at
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:48:   $a$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); and for every norm $N$ on
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:49:   $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ the real-valued function $x \mapsto N(f(x))$ is continuous at
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:50:   $a$ ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:51:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:52:**Where $m \ge 1$ is spent.** The "if" direction of clauses 1 and 2 divides
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:53:$\varepsilon$ by $\iota(m)$, which requires $\iota(m) \ne 0$; and clause 3's last
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:54:part quotes a bound available only for $m \ge 1$. The "only if" directions hold
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:55:for every $m$ but say nothing at $m = 0$, there being no index $i < 0$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:56:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:57:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:58:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:59:**Given:** A natural $m \ge 1$; a metric space $(X,d_X)$, a subset $A \subseteq X$, a point $a \in A$ and functions $f, g : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$; a real $\lambda$; and a real $\varepsilon > 0$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:60:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:61:[L1] Continuity and limits of vector-valued functions in the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ form, the coordinate projections $\pi_i$, and $|y_i| \le \lVert y\rVert_2$ for $i<m$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-metric-continuity]], [[def-metric-space]], [[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:62:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:63:[L2] The comparison $\lVert y\rVert_2 \le \lVert y\rVert_1 = \sum_{i<m}|y_i|$, and $N(y) \le C\lVert y\rVert_1 \le C\sqrt{\iota(m)}\lVert y\rVert_2$ with $C := \max\{N(e_i) : i<m\} \ge 0$, together with $|N(y)-N(z)| \le N(y-z)$, all for $m \ge 1$ ([[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]] clauses 1, 2, 3, [[def-p-norms-on-rn]], [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:64:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:65:[L3] The norm axioms for $\lVert\cdot\rVert_2$ ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]], [[lem-p-norms-are-norms-and-induce-the-published-metrics]]), and $\lVert u-v\rVert_2 = d_2(u,v)$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:66:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:67:[L4] Cauchy-Schwarz: $|\langle u,v\rangle| \le \lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$, together with bilinearity and symmetry of the inner product ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:68:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:69:[L5] Laws of finite sums ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[def-finite-sum]]): additivity, scaling, monotonicity, $\sum_{i<m}\mu = \iota(m)\mu$, a sum of nonnegative terms is nonnegative, and each single term is at most such a sum.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:70:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:71:[L6] $\iota(m) > 0$ for $m \ge 1$, and $u>0$ gives $u^{-1}>0$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]]).
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:72:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:73:[L7] A nonempty finite set of reals has a minimum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]); and a family of nonempty sets indexed by a natural number has a choice function, this being a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]), which is what licenses picking one $\delta_i$ for each $i<m$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:74:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:75:[L8] Absolute value ([[lem-of-abs-value]]): $|t| \ge 0$, $|st| = |s||t|$, and $|s+t| \le |s|+|t|$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:76:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:77:## Proof
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:78:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:79:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:80:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:81:1.1 For every $y \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$: $|y_i| \le \lVert y\rVert_2$ for each $i<m$, and $\lVert y\rVert_2 \le \sum_{i<m}|y_i|$. [L1, L2]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:82:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:83:1.2 If $a_i < b_i$ for every $i<m$ and $m \ge 1$, then $\sum_{i<m}a_i < \sum_{i<m}b_i$: the list $i \mapsto b_i - a_i$ has positive terms, so its sum is at least its term at index $0$, hence positive, and additivity gives the strict inequality. [L5, L6]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:84:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:85:1.3 For each $i<m$ the set of positive reals $\delta$ witnessing continuity of $f_i$ at $a$ for a given tolerance is nonempty whenever $f_i$ is continuous at $a$, so a choice function on the family indexed by $m$ produces $\delta_0,\dots,\delta_{m-1}$ simultaneously, with no choice principle used. [L7]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:86:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:87:1.4 For $f+g$: given $\varepsilon > 0$, pick $\delta_1, \delta_2 > 0$ for the tolerance $\varepsilon/\iota(2)$ at $f$ and at $g$ and put $\delta := \min\{\delta_1,\delta_2\}$; then for $d_X(x,a) < \delta$, $\lVert (f+g)(x)-(f+g)(a)\rVert_2 = \lVert (f(x)-f(a)) + (g(x)-g(a))\rVert_2 \le \lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 + \lVert g(x)-g(a)\rVert_2 < \varepsilon$. [L1, L3, L6, L7]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:88:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:89:1.5 For $\lambda f$: if $\lambda = 0$ then $\lambda f$ is constant and every $\delta$ serves; otherwise $|\lambda| > 0$, and a $\delta$ for the tolerance $\varepsilon/|\lambda|$ at $f$ gives $\lVert \lambda f(x) - \lambda f(a)\rVert_2 = |\lambda|\,\lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 < \varepsilon$. [L1, L3, L6, L8]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:90:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:91:1.6 For $N \circ f$: by [L2], $|N(f(x)) - N(f(a))| \le N\bigl(f(x)-f(a)\bigr) \le C\sqrt{\iota(m)}\,\lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2$; so a $\delta$ for the tolerance $\varepsilon/(C\sqrt{\iota(m)}+1)$ at $f$ serves for $N\circ f$. [L1, L2, L6]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:92:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:93:1.7 For $\langle f,g\rangle$: first take $\delta_0 > 0$ with $\lVert g(x)-g(a)\rVert_2 < 1$ for $d_X(x,a) < \delta_0$, so that $\lVert g(x)\rVert_2 \le \lVert g(x)-g(a)\rVert_2 + \lVert g(a)\rVert_2 < B := \lVert g(a)\rVert_2 + 1$ there. [L1, L3]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:94:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:95:2.1 Suppose $f$ is continuous at $a$ and fix $i<m$. Given $\varepsilon > 0$, take $\delta$ from the definition; for $x \in A$ with $d_X(x,a) < \delta$, step 1.1 gives $|f_i(x)-f_i(a)| \le \lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 < \varepsilon$. So $f_i$ is continuous at $a$. [step 1.1, L1]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:96:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:97:2.2 Conversely suppose every $f_i$ is continuous at $a$. Given $\varepsilon > 0$, the real $\varepsilon/\iota(m)$ is positive; by step 1.3 choose $\delta_i > 0$ for each $i<m$ with $|f_i(x)-f_i(a)| < \varepsilon/\iota(m)$ whenever $x \in A$ and $d_X(x,a) < \delta_i$, and put $\delta := \min\{\delta_0,\dots,\delta_{m-1}\} > 0$. [step 1.3, L1, L6, L7]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:98:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:99:2.3 By bilinearity, $\langle f(x),g(x)\rangle - \langle f(a),g(a)\rangle = \langle f(x)-f(a),\, g(x)\rangle + \langle f(a),\, g(x)-g(a)\rangle$, so Cauchy-Schwarz and step 1.7 give $\bigl|\langle f(x),g(x)\rangle - \langle f(a),g(a)\rangle\bigr| \le B\,\lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 + \lVert f(a)\rVert_2\,\lVert g(x)-g(a)\rVert_2$ for every $x \in A$ with $d_X(x,a) < \delta_0$. [step 1.7, L4, L8]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:100:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:101:3.1 For $x \in A$ with $d_X(x,a) < \delta$: each $|f_i(x)-f_i(a)| < \varepsilon/\iota(m)$, so by steps 1.1 and 1.2, $\lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 \le \sum_{i<m}|f_i(x)-f_i(a)| < \sum_{i<m}\varepsilon/\iota(m) = \varepsilon$. Hence $f$ is continuous at $a$, and clause 1 is proved. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, L5, L6]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:102:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:103:3.2 Clause 2 is the same two estimates with $f(a)$ replaced by $L$, $f_i(a)$ by $L_i$, and the condition $d_X(x,a) < \delta$ by $0 < |x-c| < \delta$: step 1.1 gives $|f_i(x)-L_i| \le \lVert f(x)-L\rVert_2$ for the forward direction, and steps 1.1, 1.2 give $\lVert f(x)-L\rVert_2 \le \sum_{i<m}|f_i(x)-L_i| < \varepsilon$ for the converse, with $\delta$ the minimum of $m$ radii obtained as in step 2.2. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, L1, L5, L6, L7]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:104:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:105:3.3 Put $P := B + \lVert f(a)\rVert_2 + 1 > 0$ and take $\delta \le \delta_0$ positive with both $\lVert f(x)-f(a)\rVert_2 < \varepsilon/P$ and $\lVert g(x)-g(a)\rVert_2 < \varepsilon/P$ for $d_X(x,a) < \delta$; then step 2.3 bounds the difference by $(B + \lVert f(a)\rVert_2)\varepsilon/P < \varepsilon$, so $\langle f,g\rangle$ is continuous at $a$. [step 1.7, step 2.3, L1, L6, L7]
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:106:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:107:4.1 Steps 1.4, 1.5, 1.6 and 3.3 are clause 3, and with steps 3.1 and 3.2 all three clauses are proved. [step 3.1, step 3.2, step 1.4, step 1.5, step 1.6, step 3.3] ∎
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:108:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:109:## Remarks
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:110:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:111:- **Why the algebra is proved here rather than quoted.** The published [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]] and [[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]] are stated for real-valued functions on a subset of $\mathbb{R}$, and the domain in clause 3 is a subset of an arbitrary metric space; quoting them for a metric domain would be a citation to an item for a claim it does not make. The estimates in steps 1.4 to 3.3 are the same ones, written out. When the domain **is** a subset of $\mathbb{R}$, clause 1 and [[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]] identify the two readings, and the published theorems may then be used on the components.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:112:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:113:- **Clause 3 is what makes the mean value inequality work.** The auxiliary function $t \mapsto \langle u, f(t)\rangle$ of [[thm-mean-value-inequality]] is continuous exactly by the inner-product part of clause 3, applied with the constant function $u$.
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:114:
items/thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity.md:115:- **Nothing here is a sequential argument, so no choice principle is used** beyond the finitely many simultaneous selections of steps 1.3, 2.2 and 3.3, which are covered by [[lem-finite-choice]], a theorem of ZF.
