items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:35:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:36:## Statement
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:37:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:38:Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, let $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ carry the Euclidean
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:39:metric $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], and let $\bigl(x^{(j)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{N}}$
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:40:be a sequence in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ ([[def-metric-convergence]]). For $k < n$ write
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:41:$\bigl(x^{(j)}_k\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{N}}$ for the $k$-th **coordinate sequence**,
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:42:a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]). Then:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:43:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:44:1. **Convergence is componentwise.** For $x \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$,
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:45:   $x^{(j)} \to x$ in $(\mathbb{R}^{n}, d_2)$ if and only if
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:46:   $x^{(j)}_k \to x_k$ in $\mathbb{R}$ for every $k<n$
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:47:   ([[def-real-limit]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:48:2. **Cauchyness is componentwise.** $\bigl(x^{(j)}\bigr)$ is Cauchy in
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:49:   $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_2)$ ([[def-cauchy-in-metric]]) if and only if every
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:50:   coordinate sequence is Cauchy in $\mathbb{R}$.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:51:3. **Completeness in every norm.** For every norm $N$ on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:52:   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]) the metric space $(\mathbb{R}^{n}, d_N)$ is
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:53:   complete ([[def-complete-metric-space]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:54:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:55:**Clause 3 is obtained by citation and is not reproved here.**
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:56:[[thm-euclidean-space-complete]] clause 2 states that $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_2)$ is
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:57:complete, **for $n \ge 1$ only**, and this theorem carries that hypothesis
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:58:forward without weakening it; what is added is the passage from $d_2$ to an
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:59:arbitrary norm, through [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]] and the dictionary
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:60:of [[def-equivalent-norms]].
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:61:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:62:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:63:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:64:**Given:** A natural $n \ge 1$; the space $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ with the norms of [[def-p-norms-on-rn]] and the metric $d_2$; a sequence $\bigl(x^{(j)}\bigr)$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$; a point $x \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$; a norm $N$ on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$; and a rational $\varepsilon > 0$.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:65:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:66:[L1] The comparison chain for $n \ge 1$ ([[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]] clause 3): $\lVert y\rVert_\infty \le \lVert y\rVert_2 \le \lVert y\rVert_1 \le \iota(n)\lVert y\rVert_\infty$ for every $y \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$, where $\lVert y\rVert_\infty = \max\{|y_k| : k<n\}$ ([[def-p-norms-on-rn]], [[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:67:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:68:[L2] The dictionary $d_2(u,v) = \lVert u-v\rVert_2$, and $(u-v)_k = u_k - v_k$ ([[lem-p-norms-are-norms-and-induce-the-published-metrics]], [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:69:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:70:[L3] Convergence and Cauchyness in a metric space, and their agreement on $\mathbb{R}$ with the real notions ([[def-metric-convergence]], [[def-cauchy-in-metric]], [[def-real-limit]], [[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]); rational and real $\varepsilon$ may be used interchangeably in both.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:71:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:72:[L4] $(\mathbb{R}^{n}, d_2)$ is complete for $n \ge 1$ ([[thm-euclidean-space-complete]] clause 2, [[def-complete-metric-space]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:73:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:74:[L5] All norms on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equivalent for $n \ge 1$ ([[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]]), and equivalent norms have the same convergent sequences with the same limits and the same Cauchy sequences ([[def-equivalent-norms]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:75:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:76:[L6] Limits in a metric space are unique, and every convergent sequence is Cauchy ([[lem-metric-limits-unique]], [[lem-metric-convergent-implies-cauchy]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:77:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:78:[L7] $\iota(n) > 0$ for $n \ge 1$, and $u > 0$ gives $u^{-1} > 0$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:79:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:80:[L8] A nonempty finite set of naturals has a greatest element, and every nonempty set of naturals has a least element ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]], [[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:81:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:82:## Proof
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:83:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:84:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:85:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:86:1.1 For every $y \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$ and every $k<n$: $|y_k| \le \lVert y\rVert_\infty \le \lVert y\rVert_2$, the first inequality because $\lVert y\rVert_\infty$ bounds the set it is the maximum of. [L1]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:87:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:88:1.2 For every $y \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$: $\lVert y\rVert_2 \le \iota(n)\lVert y\rVert_\infty$, and $\lVert y\rVert_\infty = |y_{k_0}|$ for some $k_0 < n$. [L1]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:89:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:90:1.3 Conversely suppose $x^{(j)}_k \to x_k$ for every $k<n$. Given a rational $\varepsilon > 0$, the real $\varepsilon/\iota(n)$ is positive, so for each $k<n$ the set of indices $K$ such that $|x^{(j)}_k - x_k| < \varepsilon/\iota(n)$ for all $j \ge K$ is a nonempty set of naturals; let $K_k$ be its least element, a determination rather than a selection, and put $K := \max\{K_0,\dots,K_{n-1}\}$, a maximum of a nonempty finite set of naturals. [L3, L7, L8]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:91:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:92:1.4 $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_2)$ is complete, by citation and for $n \ge 1$ only. [L4]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:93:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:94:1.5 Let $N$ be any norm on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$. By [L5], $N$ and $\lVert\cdot\rVert_2$ are equivalent, so $d_N$ and $d_2$ have the same Cauchy sequences and the same convergent sequences with the same limits. [L5]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:95:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:96:2.1 For all $u,v \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $k<n$: $|u_k - v_k| \le d_2(u,v) \le \iota(n)\max\{|u_k-v_k| : k<n\}$, by steps 1.1 and 1.2 applied to $y := u - v$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L2]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:97:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:98:2.2 Hence a Cauchy sequence in $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_N)$ is Cauchy in $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_2)$, converges there by step 1.4, and therefore converges in $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_N)$ to the same point; so $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_N)$ is complete, which is clause 3. [step 1.4, step 1.5, L5, L6]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:99:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:100:3.1 Suppose $x^{(j)} \to x$ in $(\mathbb{R}^{n},d_2)$ and fix $k<n$. Given a rational $\varepsilon > 0$, take $K$ with $d_2(x^{(j)},x) < \varepsilon$ for $j \ge K$; then $|x^{(j)}_k - x_k| \le d_2(x^{(j)},x) < \varepsilon$ for $j \ge K$, so $x^{(j)}_k \to x_k$. [step 2.1, L3]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:101:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:102:3.2 For $j \ge K$ and every $k<n$ we have $|x^{(j)}_k - x_k| < \varepsilon/\iota(n)$; the maximum of these $n$ numbers is one of them, so $\max\{|x^{(j)}_k - x_k| : k<n\} < \varepsilon/\iota(n)$ and hence $d_2(x^{(j)},x) < \iota(n)\cdot\varepsilon/\iota(n) = \varepsilon$ by step 2.1. Therefore $x^{(j)} \to x$. [step 2.1, step 1.3, L1, L7]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:103:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:104:3.3 The same two estimates prove clause 2 with $x$ replaced by $x^{(l)}$ throughout: if $d_2(x^{(j)},x^{(l)}) < \varepsilon$ for $j,l \ge K$ then $|x^{(j)}_k - x^{(l)}_k| < \varepsilon$ for $j,l \ge K$ and every $k<n$; and conversely, choosing for each $k<n$ the least $K_k$ beyond which $|x^{(j)}_k - x^{(l)}_k| < \varepsilon/\iota(n)$ for $j,l \ge K_k$ and taking $K := \max\{K_0,\dots,K_{n-1}\}$ gives $d_2(x^{(j)},x^{(l)}) < \varepsilon$ for $j,l \ge K$. [step 2.1, L3, L7, L8]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:105:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:106:4.1 Steps 3.1 and 3.2 are the two directions of clause 1. [step 3.1, step 3.2]
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:107:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:108:5.1 Clauses 1, 2 and 3 are steps 4.1, 3.3 and 2.2. [step 4.1, step 3.3, step 2.2] ∎
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:109:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:110:## Remarks
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:111:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:112:- **No choice principle is used.** The only place a family of indices is produced is steps 1.3 and 3.3, where finitely many indices are obtained, each as the **least** element of a nonempty set of naturals ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]). A least element is determined by the set, not selected from it.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:113:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:114:- **What happens at $n = 0$, stated separately because the theorem does not cover it.** $\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function, and is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); by [[def-norm-and-normed-space]] it carries exactly one norm, the zero function, whose induced metric is constantly $0$. Every sequence in a one-point metric space is Cauchy and converges to that point, so $(\mathbb{R}^{0}, d_N)$ is complete. **That statement is proved here from scratch in this remark and is not obtained from [[thm-euclidean-space-complete]]**, which is stated for $n \ge 1$ only because $d_\infty$ is a maximum over an empty index set at $n = 0$. Clauses 1 and 2 are vacuous at $n = 0$, there being no index $k<0$.
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:115:
items/thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness.md:116:- **Clause 1 is the reason the rest of this page can work coordinatewise.** Every later item that reduces a statement about $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ or $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ to $n$ or $m$ statements about $\mathbb{R}$ passes through it, and each such item therefore carries the hypothesis $n \ge 1$ or $m \ge 1$ in its own statement.
