items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:27:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:28:## Definition
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:29:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:30:Throughout, $m \in \mathbb{N}$ with $m \ge 1$, and vector-valued functions, their
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:31:components and their limits are as in
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:32:[[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]].
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:33:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:34:### The derivative
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:35:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:36:Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ and let $c \in A$ be
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:37:a **limit point of $A$** ([[def-limit-point-r]]). The **difference quotient of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:38:$f$ at $c$** is the vector-valued function
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:39:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:40:$$q_{f,c} : A \setminus \{c\} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}, \qquad q_{f,c}(x) \;:=\; \frac{1}{x - c}\,\bigl(f(x) - f(c)\bigr),$$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:41:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:42:the scalar multiple being that of the vector space $\mathbb{R}^{m}$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:43:([[def-function-space]]); the division is legitimate because $x \ne c$ gives
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:44:$x - c \ne 0$. As in [[def-derivative]], $c$ is a limit point of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:45:$A \setminus \{c\}$ as well, since a punctured neighbourhood of $c$ omits $c$.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:46:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:47:$f$ is **differentiable at $c$** when $\lim_{x \to c} q_{f,c}(x)$ exists in
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:48:$\mathbb{R}^{m}$, and then the **derivative** is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:49:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:50:$$f'(c) \;:=\; \lim_{x\to c} q_{f,c}(x) \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{m}.$$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:51:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:52:**The notation denotes a single vector.** At most one $L \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:53:satisfies the limit condition, as proved in
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:54:[[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]]; this is the vector-valued
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:55:form of the obligation [[lem-function-limit-unique]] discharges for real-valued
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:56:functions and [[lem-metric-limits-unique]] for sequences.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:57:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:58:**The intrinsic form is the definition; the componentwise form is a theorem.**
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:59:For $i < m$ the $i$-th component of $q_{f,c}(x)$ is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:60:$\bigl(f_i(x)-f_i(c)\bigr)/(x-c)$, which is the real difference quotient of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:61:$f_i$ at $c$ ([[def-derivative]]). So by
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:62:[[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]] clause 2:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:63:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:64:> $f$ is differentiable at $c$ **if and only if** every $f_i$ is differentiable
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:65:> at $c$, and then $f'(c)_i = f_i'(c)$ for every $i<m$.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:66:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:67:Nothing below reverses this order of presentation: the intrinsic limit is what is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:68:defined, and the coordinates are read off it.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:69:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:70:**Algebra of derivatives.** If $f, g : A \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ are differentiable at
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:71:$c$ and $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f + g$ and $\lambda f$ are differentiable
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:72:at $c$ with $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c)+g'(c)$ and $(\lambda f)'(c) = \lambda f'(c)$: read
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:73:componentwise through the displayed equivalence, these are clauses 1 and 2 of the
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:74:published [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]].
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:75:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:76:### The integral
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:77:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:78:Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:79:([[def-interval]]). $f$ is **integrable on $[a,b]$** when every component
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:80:$f_i : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Darboux
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:81:integrable in the sense of [[def-darboux-integral]], and then
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:82:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:83:$$\int_a^b f \;:=\; \text{the function } m \to \mathbb{R} \text{ sending } i \mapsto \int_a^b f_i .$$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:84:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:85:**That really is an element of $\mathbb{R}^{m}$.** In this library
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:86:$\mathbb{R}^{m}$ is the set of functions $m \to \mathbb{R}$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:87:([[def-function-space]]), not a set of tuples, so the displayed assignment is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:88:literally an element of it; each value $\int_a^b f_i$ is a single real by
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:89:[[def-darboux-integral]]. In the standard basis
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:90:([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]) the same object is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:91:$\int_a^b f = \sum_{i<m}\bigl(\int_a^b f_i\bigr)e_i$.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:92:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:93:**Oriented limits.** Following [[def-oriented-integral]] componentwise, set
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:94:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:95:$$\int_a^a f \;:=\; 0 \in \mathbb{R}^{m}, \qquad \int_b^a f \;:=\; -\int_a^b f \quad (a < b),$$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:96:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:97:so that $\int_u^v f = -\int_v^u f$ for all $u,v$ in an interval on which $f$ is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:98:integrable. The clauses do not overlap with the case $a<b$, so nothing has to be
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:99:checked for consistency, exactly as in [[def-oriented-integral]].
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:100:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:101:**Linearity.** If $f, g : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ are integrable and
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:102:$\lambda,\mu \in \mathbb{R}$ then $\lambda f + \mu g$ is integrable with
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:103:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:104:$$\int_a^b (\lambda f + \mu g) \;=\; \lambda\int_a^b f + \mu\int_a^b g ,$$
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:105:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:106:since each side has $i$-th coordinate $\int_a^b(\lambda f_i + \mu g_i)$ and
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:107:$\lambda\int_a^b f_i + \mu\int_a^b g_i$ respectively, and those agree by
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:108:[[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]].
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:109:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:110:**Restriction and splitting.** If $f$ is integrable on $[a,b]$ then it is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:111:integrable on every closed subinterval, and for $a<c<b$,
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:112:$\int_a^b f = \int_a^c f + \int_c^b f$; both are the componentwise readings of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:113:[[lem-integrability-on-a-subinterval]] and
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:114:[[thm-additivity-over-subintervals]], applied to each $f_i$ and reassembled
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:115:coordinate by coordinate.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:116:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:117:## Remarks
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:118:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:119:- **The two halves are independent.** The derivative clause needs no integral and
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:120:  the integral clause needs no derivative; they are collected in one item because
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:121:  they are the two constructions of the one-dimensional theory that transfer to
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:122:  $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ by the same move, and because
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:123:  [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] is what joins them.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:124:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:125:- **Why the intrinsic derivative is stated first.** The componentwise formula is
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:126:  the one used in computations, but it is tied to the standard basis, whereas the
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:127:  limit of the difference quotient is not. The intrinsic form is the one that
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:128:  survives when the domain is enlarged from an interval to a subset of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:129:  $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is a later page of this track.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:130:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:131:- **No norm appears in either definition.** The derivative is a limit in
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:132:  $(\mathbb{R}^{m}, d_2)$, and by [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]] the same
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:133:  limits are obtained from any other norm, so the notion does not depend on the
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:134:  choice ([[def-p-norms-on-rn]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). The integral
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:135:  is defined coordinatewise and mentions no metric at all.
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:136:
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:137:- **Integrability of $\lVert f\rVert_2$ is a separate matter** and is not part of
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:138:  this definition: it is proved, together with the inequality it belongs to, in
items/def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:139:  [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]].
