**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 The three coordinate sequences of $\bigl(x^{(k)}\bigr)$ are $k \mapsto 1/\iota(k+1)$, $k \mapsto 1 - 1/\iota(k+1)$ and the constant $2$. Given a [-rational-]{+real+} $\varepsilon>0$, take $K$ with $1/\iota(K+1)<\varepsilon$; then for $k \ge K$ one has $0 < 1/\iota(k+1) \le 1/\iota(K+1) < \varepsilon$, so the first converges to $0$, the second to $1$ and the third to $2$. [L2]

1.2 Each component of $f$ is a polynomial function, hence continuous on $[0,1]$ and integrable there; so $f$ is integrable, and $f$ is continuous. [L3, L4, L9]

